-- title:
--   §3, PART 1, p. 9 — $S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}\subseteq\widetilde S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal X\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ and $\mathcal X_\delta\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ ($\delta\in\Delta$) be convex, let $c\in\mathbb R^d$, and suppose that for every number $k$ of constraints and every $(\delta^{(1)},\dots,\delta^{(k)})\in\Delta^k$ the program $P_k$ (minimise $c^\top x$ over $x\in\mathcal X\cap\bigcap_{i}\mathcal X_{\delta^{(i)}}$) has a unique solution $x^*_k$. Fix $m\ge d$. Write $S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}$ for the set of multi-extractions $(\delta^{(1)},\dots,\delta^{(m)})$ whose support constraints are exactly $\delta^{(1)},\dots,\delta^{(d)}$, and $\widetilde S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}$ for the set where $\delta^{(d+1)},\dots,\delta^{(m)}$ are not violated by the solution generated by $\delta^{(1)},\dots,\delta^{(d)}$. Then
--   $$S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}\subseteq\widetilde S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}.$$
--
--   This is the first half of the fact, stated on p. 8 and proved at the end of PART 1 (p. 9), that $S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}$ and $\widetilde S_{\bar{\mathcal I}}$ coincide up to a probability-zero set. It holds for every multi-extraction; no probability enters. The paper's step "the first $d$ constraints are still the support constraints for the problem with $m-1$ constraints" uses Proposition 2.2 (at most $d$ support constraints), which is why convexity is assumed.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The paper leaves implicit that its Assumption 1 holds for every number of constraints; we state existence and uniqueness for every $k$ and every sample, and take a solution family `θs k ω` assumed to solve $P_k$ (so `θs k ω` is $x^*_k$). Support constraints are the published `IsSupportConstraint` (removal admits a feasible point of strictly smaller cost), which under uniqueness is the paper's "its removal changes the solution". Closedness, measurability and full support are not needed and not assumed. Indexes are 0-based.
-- source:
--   Campi & Garatti, The exact feasibility of randomized solutions of uncertain convex programs, SIAM J. Optim. 19(3) (2008); authors' final manuscript, p. 9, 'Proof of the fact that S_𝓘̄ = S̃_𝓘̄ up to a probability zero set', S_𝓘̄ ⊆ S̃_𝓘̄ (claim stated p. 8)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ScenarioApproach_Generalization_violation
import Definitions.Def_ScenarioApproach_Generalization_scenarioProgram
import Definitions.Def_ScenarioApproach_Generalization_supportConstraint
import Definitions.Def_ScenarioExact_PartOne_Basic
open MeasureTheory ScenarioApproach.Generalization
open scoped ENNReal

namespace ScenarioExact.PartOne

/-- §3, PART 1, p. 9 (claim on p. 8): `S_𝓘̄ ⊆ S̃_𝓘̄`. Deterministic: for every multi-extraction
whose support constraints are exactly the first `d`, the solution generated by the first `d`
constraints violates none of the others. -/
theorem subset_Stilde {d m : ℕ} {Δ : Type*}
    (c : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (Θ : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)))
    (Θδ : Δ → Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)))
    (hΘ_convex : Convex ℝ Θ) (hΘδ_convex : ∀ δ, Convex ℝ (Θδ δ))
    (hexu : ∀ (k : ℕ) (ω : Fin k → Δ), ∃! θ, IsSolution c Θ Θδ ω θ)
    (θs : (k : ℕ) → (Fin k → Δ) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (hθs : ∀ (k : ℕ) (ω : Fin k → Δ), IsSolution c Θ Θδ ω (θs k ω))
    (hm : d ≤ m) :
    S c Θ Θδ θs (Ibar d m) ⊆ Stilde Θδ θs hm := by sorry

end ScenarioExact.PartOne
